Got it—single car only, no alternatives. Let’s look at insurance for your Mazda 3 commuter setup.

The cheapest path is usually a “liability-only” policy: state minimums for bodily injury and property damage. In many states that lands around $80–$120/month for a single clean-record driver, but mileage and location matter a lot.

If you add collision and comprehensive, expect to pay an extra $30–$60/month depending on the deductible you pick (higher deductibles bring the premium down).

You can trim costs even further by:
- Raising your deductible to $500+;
- Asking for the multi-vehicle or good-driver discount even if you don’t qualify for bundling;
- Paying annually instead of month-to-month to avoid installment fees;
- Asking your workplace if they offer a telematics discount for safe driving.

A $300 monthly total budget for running costs is tight but doable. Insurance alone may eat $80–$120, leaving $180–$220 for gas, maintenance, and registration. If your commute is under 20 miles each way, that’s usually enough, assuming you budget for occasional oil changes and tire wear.

